course: dictionary
lesson: 13614
title: Words that mean impossible to pin down
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Indeterminable is a long, formal word. Break it into parts: in- means not, determine means to decide or find out for sure, and -able means can be. So indeterminable means cannot be found out or decided exactly.
explain: «The rain had washed the writing away, so the message was indeterminable.» The clues are washed away and rain. The writing is gone, so nobody can find out what it said. Indeterminable means impossible to find out.
explain: Try the swap test. Put «impossible to find out exactly» into the sentence in place of the hard word. «The message was impossible to find out exactly» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Indeterminable means impossible to find out, measure or decide exactly.
fact: It comes from determine, which means to decide or find out for sure, and the ending -able means can be done.
fact: It is a formal word, so you will see it in science, maths and history books more often than in chatty talk.
fact: «The exact age of the fossil is indeterminable» means that even careful checking cannot give one exact answer.
fact: The opposite idea is «easy to measure» or «clearly known».
exercise: «The exact age of the damaged fossil is indeterminable.» What does indeterminable mean here? | Written in red ink | *Impossible to find out exactly | Very old and very shiny | Easy to measure with a ruler
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to indeterminable? | Bright and colourful | Loud and noisy | Simple and quick | *Impossible to decide exactly
exercise: «The question gave too little information, so the answer was indeterminable.» Why is the answer indeterminable? | It was a very short question | *There was not enough information | The maths teacher was too strict | The answer is written in big letters
test: Which word fits? The exact birth date of those ancient people is now ___. | *indeterminable | Quick | Loud | Shiny
test: «Mia tried to measure the mist, but its edges kept moving, so its size was indeterminable.» What does this tell us? | *The mist could not be measured exactly | Mia measured the mist perfectly | The mist was very large | Mia gave up on the mist
exam: Which sentence uses indeterminable correctly? | I finished my sandwich, so the sandwich was indeterminable | The song was loud, so the song was indeterminable | *The coin was so worn that its date was indeterminable | The ruler showed exactly 12 centimetres, so the length was indeterminable
